title: 'pallet-revive: make the first hot write after a cold read pay the deferred
  cost'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    ### Summary
    A slot warmed by a read and then written was charged only the transaction-time work, while the extra cost a write has  over a read was skipped: re-encoding and re-hashing the slot's trie path when the block's storage root is computed.

    The access list now tracks per slot whether the transaction has paid the read cost or the write cost. The first write to a  read-paid slot pays the write surcharge and upgrades the slot to write-paid. Upgrades are journaled per frame, like insertions, and roll back with a reverting frame since the reverted write leaves no root work behind.

    `AccessList` holds a `BTreeMap<AccessEntry, StorageOp>` in place of the bounded set, plus a second journal for the upgrades. `MAX_ACCESS_LIST_ENTRIES` is enforced at the insertion point rather than by the type, since `BoundedBTreeMap` has no `entry` API and would make every cold touch search the map twice. The worst-case per-entry memory constant grows from 512 to 768 bytes, all of it for a slot's upgrade-journal entry; an entry measures the same in the map as it did in the set, since the one-byte value does not push a B-tree node into the allocator's next size class.

    ### Pricing notes
    - The surcharge is ref_time-only; the root re-hash adds no proof.
    - The surcharge can be paid twice when a frame reverts: the reverted frame keeps its charge, the upgrade rolls back, and the slot's next write pays it again.
    - The rollback work of draining upgrades is not charged separately: the reverted write already paid a surcharge for block-end work that will never happen.
    - The `access_list_touch_hot_full` benchmark now measures the worst case, the largest key in a full map and a write that upgrades a read-paid slot.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: major
- name: asset-hub-westend-runtime
  bump: patch
